Pawpoint is the software the front desk of Hillside Veterinary Clinic runs on all day. Rosa, the receptionist, uses it to see who is in, who is waiting and who is next, to check patients in as they arrive, to book follow-ups while the owner is standing at the counter, and to pull up a pet's record when a vet asks.

At `/`, today's schedule: every appointment for the three vets with its time, patient, owner, reason and status (booked, checked in, in exam, done, cancelled). Urgent visits stand out. Rosa can check a booked patient in, and the waiting list (who is checked in and for how long) updates. She can book a new appointment by picking a patient, a vet and a time; it then shows on the schedule, and it cannot clash with an existing booking for that vet. A search finds a patient or owner by name and opens the record.

At `/patients/p-104`, Olive's record: patient and owner details, the alerts, a weight trend over her visits, vaccinations with the overdue ones flagged against today's date, current medications and the visit history with notes. Medical alerts must be hard to miss wherever a patient appears.

It is used on a tablet at the counter and sometimes on a phone.

Style: up to you. It should look and feel like software the people above would pay for and use all day; no style guide is given, so the visual choices are yours.

